# Pokemon Stadium 2 — fair-heavy (realistic ~7% edits, not adversarial)
# 64 MB N64 ROM. Sits one notch heavier than dm4y (4 MB, real
# translation): meaningfully heavy work for BPS to do, but with edit
# shapes a real ROM hack might make (block swaps, region copies,
# structured tables, masked transforms).

base:   test/data/stadium2/base.z64
sha1:   f3b9f582c97d644c023622973ca04ef3b5a87dfc
desc:   stadium2 fair-heavy (64 MB N64, ~7% changed, realistic shapes)

BPS      | test/data/stadium2/fair-heavy/patch.bps         | synthetic | slap
IPS32    | test/data/stadium2/fair-heavy/patch.ips32       | synthetic | slap
UPS      | test/data/stadium2/fair-heavy/patch.ups         | synthetic | slap
PPF3     | test/data/stadium2/fair-heavy/patch.ppf         | synthetic | slap
NINJA1   | test/data/stadium2/fair-heavy/patch.ninja1      | synthetic | slap
NINJA2   | test/data/stadium2/fair-heavy/patch.rup         | synthetic | slap
APS-N64  | test/data/stadium2/fair-heavy/patch.aps         | synthetic | slap
GDIFF    | test/data/stadium2/fair-heavy/patch.gdiff       | synthetic | slap

# slap made each of these. For each format we also made a patch with
# the listed tool, and applied them with both tools — the resulting
# SHAs all agreed.
#
#   IPS32   sips (create), Atmosphère (apply)*
#   UPS     Go ups
#   PPF3    MakePPF3
#   NINJA1  ninja-1.01php**
#   NINJA2  ninja-2.0 PHP reference***
#   APS-N64 RomPatcher.js
#   GDIFF   javaxdelta
#
# *   We used their apply algorithm, not the program directly
# **  PHP only encodes {raw, snes, mega, gb} and its >30 MB streaming
#     encoder has a stale-$pointer bug. We did the four-quadrant cross-
#     apply in raw mode using a one-line bug-patched tmp/ copy of
#     ninja.php; the original docs/ tarball is untouched. Slap then
#     rebuilt the fixture with --rom-type n64 (single-byte diff at
#     header byte 8: 0x00 → 0x03).
# *** Mode 1 (UTF-8 PATCH_ENC), --type n64 on both sides. NINJA2's
#     n64_read is a no-op pass-through for canonical .z64 input
#     (magic 80 37 12 40), so both encoders diff the same byte stream
#     and both write 0x04 directly in the rom-type slot at offset
#     0x802. The 2006 ninja-2.0 PHP script needs two small fixes to
#     run on PHP 8: dl()/extension_loaded branch logic was inverted
#     (the script die()s when GMP/mbstring ARE loaded), and ninja_block
#     used " " as a single-file-mode filename placeholder that leaked
#     into later filesize() calls with a trailing space and broke
#     every type's create path. Both fixed in a tmp/ copy; the
#     original docs/ tarball is untouched.
